insert into public.flashcards(id,category,front,back,explanation,difficulty,display_order,published) values
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001','Assertiveness','In a project review, a colleague says your work is not good enough but gives no details. What would you say to get feedback you can act on?','Can you give me a specific example?','A request for an example turns a broad judgment into something you can examine and improve. Remember: ask for specifics before defending your work.','beginner',1,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002','Assertiveness','In a planning meeting, a senior colleague repeatedly interrupts before you finish your proposal. What would you say to finish your point while keeping the discussion constructive?','I’d like to finish this point, then hear your perspective.','You hold your turn and give the colleague a clear opening to contribute. Remember: finish your point; make room for theirs.','beginner',2,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003','Assertiveness','You are already committed to two urgent deliverables when a colleague asks you to take on another task. What would you say to make the capacity tradeoff clear?','I can take this on if we move another priority. Which should shift?','Asking which priority should move makes the cost of new work visible without rejecting the request outright. Remember: a new commitment needs a priority decision.','beginner',3,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000004','Assertiveness','A stakeholder wants a complete analysis by Friday, but the available time supports only the core findings. What would you say to set a credible deadline?','I can deliver the core analysis by Friday. The full scope needs until Tuesday.','Separating the essential result from the full scope gives the stakeholder a usable choice and protects your commitment. Remember: offer what you can reliably deliver.','beginner',4,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005','Assertiveness','During a decision meeting, the group moves past your recommendation without discussing the risk it addresses. What would you say to bring that concern back into the conversation?','Before we move on, I’d like us to consider the risk this addresses.','Connecting your point to a decision risk gives the group a reason to reconsider it. Remember: return to the consequence, not the slight.','beginner',5,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006','Assertiveness','In a team discussion, a colleague tells others what you think before you have spoken. What would you say to share your own position?','Let me share my own view on that.','A brief statement of ownership lets you correct the record without arguing about the colleague''s intentions. Remember: state your own view directly.','intermediate',6,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007','Assertiveness','Your team favors an approach that you believe will create problems for customers. What would you say to disagree constructively?','I see it differently. My concern is the impact on our customers.','Naming the customer impact grounds your disagreement in a shared outcome rather than a personal preference. Remember: disagree through the impact on the work.','intermediate',7,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000008','Assertiveness','A colleague sends a non-emergency request late in the evening and asks for a quick turnaround. What would you say to clarify urgency and your availability?','I’ll review this tomorrow morning. If it’s urgent, please clarify the deadline.','A clear response time sets a boundary while leaving room for a genuinely urgent need to be identified. Remember: state when you can respond.','intermediate',8,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000009','Assertiveness','A colleague announces in a meeting that you will own a new task before discussing it with you. What would you say before accepting the commitment?','Let’s confirm scope and priority before I commit.','Confirming scope and priority prevents an assumption from becoming an unexamined obligation. Remember: clarify before committing.','intermediate',9,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010','Assertiveness','A meeting is about to end, and your analysis includes a point that may change the next step. What would you say to secure a brief opening?','I have one point that could change the next step. May I add it?','Explaining that your input affects the next step makes your request relevant to the group''s immediate goal. Remember: make the reason to hear you clear.','intermediate',10,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011','Empathy','A colleague is frustrated because a project delay is affecting their team''s work. What would you say before offering a solution?','What is this delay making harder for you?','Asking about the impact helps you understand their actual difficulty instead of solving the wrong problem. Remember: understand the impact first.','beginner',1,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012','Empathy','A teammate says they are overwhelmed, but you do not yet know which part of the workload is hardest. What would you say to understand where support is needed?','Which part feels hardest right now?','A focused question makes a broad feeling easier to discuss and reveals where help could matter. Remember: find the hardest part before offering help.','beginner',2,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013','Empathy','You give a colleague feedback, and they quickly explain why the situation was not their fault. What would you say to make room for their perspective?','I can see this is difficult to hear. What’s your perspective?','Acknowledging that feedback can be difficult reduces pressure and invites information you may have missed. Remember: listen before debating the explanation.','beginner',3,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014','Empathy','A teammate''s error creates rework, and people start assigning blame before the facts are clear. What would you say to begin a useful conversation?','Let’s understand what happened before deciding what to change.','Understanding the sequence of events helps the team identify a fix rather than settle on a scapegoat. Remember: start with what happened.','beginner',4,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000015','Empathy','A normally engaged teammate stays quiet through several discussions today. What would you say to check in without putting them on the spot?','I noticed you’ve been quieter today. Would you like to check in privately?','A private invitation shows care while allowing them to decide whether and how much to share. Remember: offer a private opening, not a demand.','beginner',5,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000016','Empathy','A stakeholder reacts strongly to a proposed process change, although the benefits seem clear to you. What would you say to understand their concern?','What are you most concerned about losing with this change?','Asking what they may lose surfaces the practical or emotional cost that a benefits-only pitch can miss. Remember: explore what the change puts at risk.','intermediate',6,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000017','Empathy','A colleague tells you that a difficult personal situation is affecting their work. What would you say to offer useful support without prying?','Thank you for telling me. What support at work would help?','An open question about workplace support lets them set the boundary and define what would help. Remember: let the person define useful support.','intermediate',7,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000018','Empathy','After a long discussion, you think a stakeholder''s main concern is timing, but you are not certain. What would you say to check your understanding?','It sounds like your main concern is timing. Is that right?','Reflecting your interpretation gives them a chance to correct it before you respond to the wrong concern. Remember: check the meaning before acting on it.','intermediate',8,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000019','Empathy','A colleague reaches a very different conclusion from yours about a customer issue. What would you say to understand the experience behind their view?','What experience led you to that conclusion?','Asking about their experience reveals context and evidence without assuming poor judgment or motives. Remember: be curious about the basis of their view.','intermediate',9,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000020','Empathy','You offer to help a teammate, and they say they would rather handle the task themselves. What would you say to respect their decision and keep support available?','Understood. If that changes, you can let me know.','Accepting their choice preserves autonomy while leaving a clear path to ask for help later. Remember: offer support without taking over.','intermediate',10,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000021','Executive Presence','A senior leader asks for a project update with only a minute available before the next agenda item. What would you say to give a clear executive summary?','We’re on track for Friday. The main risk is approval, and I’m addressing it today.','Status, risk, and action tell the leader what is happening and whether intervention is needed. Remember: lead with the result and the next action.','beginner',1,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000022','Executive Presence','You are asked a difficult question in a meeting and feel pressure to answer immediately. What would you say to create a moment to think?','Let me take a moment to think through that.','A deliberate pause gives you time to form a useful answer and signals that you are considering the question. Remember: a brief pause is part of a clear answer.','beginner',2,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000023','Executive Presence','An executive asks for a figure you have not verified, and others are waiting for your answer. What would you say to stay credible without guessing?','I don’t have that figure yet. I’ll verify it and follow up by tomorrow.','Stating the gap and a follow-up time protects accuracy and makes your next commitment explicit. Remember: be clear about what you know and when you will know more.','beginner',3,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000024','Executive Presence','Your presentation is running long, and the discussion is drifting into background detail. What would you say to refocus the room?','The decision we need today is whether to proceed with the pilot.','Naming the decision helps listeners distinguish essential information from material that can wait. Remember: bring the room back to the decision.','beginner',4,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000025','Executive Presence','You have compared two options and need to present your recommendation before explaining the analysis. What would you say to make your position clear?','I recommend option B because it reduces risk while meeting the deadline.','A recommendation followed by its main reason gives listeners an organizing point for the details. Remember: recommendation first; supporting detail next.','beginner',5,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000026','Executive Presence','Several people raise unrelated issues in a meeting, and the original purpose is getting lost. What would you say to restore focus?','Let’s return to the outcome we’re trying to achieve.','Returning to the desired outcome creates a shared frame for deciding which topics belong now. Remember: use the outcome to organize the discussion.','intermediate',6,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000027','Executive Presence','A fast-moving meeting makes you feel you must fit every detail into one turn. What would you say to communicate one important point?','My key point is this: we need one accountable owner.','One clear message is easier to understand and remember than several hurried qualifications. Remember: choose the point that matters most.','intermediate',7,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000028','Executive Presence','An executive challenges an assumption behind your proposal during a review. What would you say to respond with composure and rigor?','That assumption is worth testing. Here’s how we can validate it.','Proposing a way to test the assumption shifts the exchange from defending yourself to improving the decision. Remember: turn a challenge into a testable question.','intermediate',8,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000029','Executive Presence','Your project misses a milestone, and you need to explain the setback to stakeholders. What would you say to acknowledge the miss and restore direction?','We missed the milestone. Here’s the recovery plan and revised date.','A recovery plan shows that you accept responsibility and have identified the next concrete steps. Remember: name the miss and the recovery plan.','intermediate',9,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000030','Executive Presence','A meeting ends with broad agreement, but no one has summarized the decision or the next owner. What would you say to close the loop?','We’ve agreed on the pilot. Priya owns the next step, due Friday.','A decision, owner, and date reduce ambiguity about what happens after the meeting. Remember: close with who does what by when.','intermediate',10,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031','Conflict Management','A disagreement about a proposal turns into comments about each other''s abilities. What would you say to redirect the exchange?','Let’s focus on the proposal and its impact, rather than on each other.','Focusing on the proposal separates an assessable work issue from personal judgments that escalate tension. Remember: discuss the work, not each other''s character.','beginner',1,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000032','Conflict Management','Two colleagues advocate different solutions and keep repeating why their own choice is better. What would you say to create a fair comparison?','What criteria would help us compare these options fairly?','Shared criteria give both options the same test and move the discussion beyond competing preferences. Remember: agree on the criteria before choosing.','beginner',2,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000033','Conflict Management','A colleague uses a sharp tone while raising an issue you both need to resolve. What would you say to set a constructive tone?','I want to resolve this. Can we discuss the concern calmly?','A calm request preserves the goal of resolving the issue while making respectful discussion an explicit expectation. Remember: set a calm condition for continuing.','beginner',3,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000034','Conflict Management','You and a stakeholder have repeated the same arguments across several meetings without progress. What would you say to identify a way forward?','What information would help us move beyond this point?','Asking what information would help exposes the uncertainty that more repetition will not resolve. Remember: look for the missing information.','beginner',4,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000035','Conflict Management','A teammate misses a deadline partly because your original instructions were unclear. What would you say to take responsibility for your part?','I wasn’t clear about the deadline. I’ll clarify the expectation now.','A specific acknowledgment removes ambiguity about your contribution and makes correction possible. Remember: own your part precisely.','beginner',5,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000036','Conflict Management','A colleague says their input was ignored in a decision, and you believe it was considered. What would you say before explaining your own account?','Which input did you feel we missed? Let’s revisit it.','Asking which input they mean gives you a concrete issue to revisit rather than an accusation to debate. Remember: get the specific concern first.','intermediate',6,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000037','Conflict Management','An unresolved conflict between two people starts consuming a meeting intended for another decision. What would you say to protect the agenda and address the conflict?','This needs a focused conversation. Let’s set aside time after this meeting.','A separate conversation gives the issue appropriate attention without making the whole group sit through it. Remember: move the conflict to a focused setting.','intermediate',7,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000038','Conflict Management','You and another team disagree on what should be prioritized, and both describe your request as urgent. What would you say to understand the underlying interests?','What outcome are we each trying to protect?','Asking about protected outcomes reveals why each priority matters and may expose a workable tradeoff. Remember: find the outcome behind the position.','intermediate',8,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000039','Conflict Management','A difficult conversation reaches a stalemate even though some practical steps are still possible. What would you say to make limited progress?','Can we agree on one next step, even if we don’t agree on everything?','One agreed action can reduce the problem while leaving room to resolve the remaining disagreement later. Remember: seek a workable next step.','intermediate',9,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000040','Conflict Management','A colleague has missed the same delivery agreement twice, and your team is absorbing the impact. What would you say to address the pattern?','We agreed on Friday delivery, and it’s slipped twice. What needs to change?','Observable dates and repeated slips make the issue concrete without labeling the person''s character. Remember: name the pattern and ask what must change.','intermediate',10,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000041','Managing Your Boss','Your boss gives you two urgent assignments that cannot both be finished today. What would you say to get a clear priority?','If I can complete only one today, which matters most?','A forced choice makes the constraint visible and places the priority decision with the person setting it. Remember: ask which outcome comes first.','beginner',1,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000042','Managing Your Boss','Your boss changes the project direction after the team has already started the original plan. What would you say to clarify the new expectations?','What has changed, and what should we stop doing as a result?','Understanding the reason and what stops prevents the old and new plans from competing for the same capacity. Remember: a change in direction needs a change in priorities.','beginner',2,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000043','Managing Your Boss','You want feedback from your boss after several meetings in which your contribution felt less effective than intended. What would you say to get actionable guidance?','What is one behavior I could change to be more effective in these meetings?','Asking for one behavior in a specific setting makes it easier to receive advice you can practice. Remember: ask for a behavior you can change.','beginner',3,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000044','Managing Your Boss','Your boss proposes a timeline that you believe carries a significant delivery risk. What would you say to raise the risk and offer another approach?','I see a risk with the timeline. May I walk you through an alternative?','A concrete concern and an alternative make your disagreement useful to the decision rather than merely oppositional. Remember: pair the concern with a feasible alternative.','beginner',4,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000045','Managing Your Boss','A decision is due today, your boss is unavailable, and you have previously agreed which choices you may make independently. What would you say to communicate your intended action?','I plan to proceed with option A unless you flag a concern by 3 p.m.','A clear plan and a response deadline create visibility while using the decision authority already agreed. Remember: act within agreed authority and make the plan visible.','beginner',5,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000046','Managing Your Boss','Your boss asks for an unexpected update, and the project needs a scope decision to stay on schedule. What would you say to make the required support clear?','The deliverable is on schedule. I need your decision on scope by Thursday.','Combining the status with a specific decision request tells your boss where their involvement matters. Remember: make the needed decision explicit.','intermediate',6,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000047','Managing Your Boss','You are working hard in a new role but are unsure what your boss will use to judge success. What would you say to align on expectations?','What would successful performance look like over the next three months?','A time-bounded definition of success turns broad expectations into a target you can discuss and track. Remember: agree on what success looks like.','intermediate',7,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000048','Managing Your Boss','Your boss praises a completed project but does not explain which part was most valuable. What would you say to learn what to repeat?','Thank you. Which part should I repeat on the next project?','A specific follow-up turns encouragement into insight you can apply on the next assignment. Remember: find the repeatable behavior behind the praise.','intermediate',8,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000049','Managing Your Boss','You discover that a pending approval could push launch back by two days. What would you say to alert your boss before the delay becomes unavoidable?','I want to flag this early: approval may delay launch by two days.','Early notice gives your boss time to help and prevents a known risk from becoming a surprise. Remember: flag material risks while there is time to act.','intermediate',9,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000050','Managing Your Boss','You want to lead an upcoming pilot but do not know what evidence your boss needs to support you. What would you say to connect the opportunity with readiness?','I’d like to own the next pilot. What would you need to see to support that?','Asking for support criteria turns ambition into a practical discussion about demonstrating capability. Remember: ask what would establish readiness.','intermediate',10,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000051','Managing Your Team','A team member is starting a task and needs direction without having every step prescribed. What would you say to define success and preserve autonomy?','The outcome is a one-page recommendation by Friday. You can choose the approach.','A clear output and deadline establish accountability while allowing them to choose an effective method. Remember: define the outcome, not every move.','beginner',1,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000052','Managing Your Team','A team member misses a deadline, and you need to understand the cause before setting a new date. What would you say to start the recovery conversation?','What got in the way, and what is a realistic recovery date?','Learning the blocker helps you negotiate a credible recovery plan instead of simply demanding more effort. Remember: diagnose the blocker before resetting the date.','beginner',2,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000053','Managing Your Team','A capable teammate brings every decision to you instead of offering their own recommendation. What would you say to build their judgment?','What do you recommend, and what tradeoff did you consider?','Asking for a recommendation and its tradeoff invites them to practice reasoning rather than wait for instructions. Remember: ask for their recommendation before giving yours.','beginner',3,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000054','Managing Your Team','You delegate an important draft and want visibility without taking ownership back from the teammate. What would you say to establish an appropriate checkpoint?','You own the draft. Let’s check progress on Wednesday before the final review.','A named owner and an interim review create support and accountability without constant supervision. Remember: delegate ownership with a clear checkpoint.','beginner',4,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000055','Managing Your Team','A teammate produces a risk summary that makes a difficult group decision much easier. What would you say to recognize the contribution?','Your clear risk summary helped us make the decision faster.','Naming the behavior and its effect shows exactly what was valuable and what to repeat. Remember: recognize the specific contribution and impact.','beginner',5,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000056','Managing Your Team','A team member repeatedly interrupts others during a discussion you are leading. What would you say in private to give useful corrective feedback?','In today’s meeting, the interruptions made it hard for others to contribute. Please let them finish.','An observable behavior, its impact, and a clear request make the feedback easier to act on. Remember: describe the behavior and the change needed.','intermediate',6,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000057','Managing Your Team','A teammate has more commitments than they can reasonably complete this week. What would you say to address the workload?','Let’s review your commitments and decide what to defer.','Reviewing and deferring commitments treats capacity as a planning issue rather than a personal failure. Remember: reduce competing commitments, not just symptoms.','intermediate',7,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000058','Managing Your Team','A new teammate has useful expertise but seems hesitant to speak in a group meeting. What would you say to invite their input without pressure?','I’d like your perspective. You can also send it after the meeting.','Offering a spoken or written route lets them contribute without requiring an immediate public response. Remember: offer more than one way to contribute.','intermediate',8,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000059','Managing Your Team','You make a decision that changes the team''s work, but people do not understand the reasoning. What would you say to provide useful context?','I’ll explain the decision, the reason, and what it means for our work.','The decision, reason, and practical implications help people understand both the direction and their next actions. Remember: explain what changed and why it matters.','intermediate',9,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000060','Managing Your Team','You realize your scope decision created avoidable rework for the team. What would you say to acknowledge the error and correct it?','I made the wrong call on scope. I’ll correct it and update the plan today.','Owning the specific call shows accountability and makes the recovery action clear. Remember: own the decision and lead the correction.','intermediate',10,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000061','Peer Management','A peer asks for help while you are working toward your own deadline. What would you say to offer help within your available time?','I can help for thirty minutes today. What is the highest-priority piece?','A time limit and a priority question focus the help on what is most valuable and feasible. Remember: make the boundary and the offer clear.','beginner',1,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000062','Peer Management','A peer shares a successful project update that leaves out your team''s contribution. What would you say to acknowledge the shared work?','I’m glad this landed well. Let’s also acknowledge the analysis our team contributed.','Adding the contribution to the success story makes the record more accurate without disputing the achievement. Remember: ask for accurate shared recognition.','beginner',2,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000063','Peer Management','You need a peer to review two questions before your team can make a release decision. What would you say to make the request easy to act on?','Could you review these two questions by noon? They’re blocking the release decision.','A defined task, time, and consequence reduce the effort needed to understand and prioritize the request. Remember: specify the ask, deadline, and impact.','beginner',3,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000064','Peer Management','You discover that you and a peer have both started solving the same problem. What would you say to prevent further duplicated effort?','Let’s agree on ownership so we don’t both solve the same problem.','An ownership conversation creates a clear division of work before more time is lost. Remember: agree on ownership early.','beginner',4,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000065','Peer Management','A peer keeps sending requests outside your agreed process, creating rework for your team. What would you say to reset the working agreement?','Can we revisit how requests come in? The current process is creating rework.','Naming the process and its effect keeps the conversation on a fixable pattern rather than personal intent. Remember: address the process causing the friction.','beginner',5,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000066','Peer Management','You are beginning a project with a peer whose team''s priorities you do not yet understand. What would you say to build a useful working relationship?','What are your team’s main priorities this quarter?','Asking about their goals gives you context for future requests and potential areas of collaboration. Remember: learn their priorities before making your ask.','intermediate',6,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000067','Peer Management','A peer gives feedback that reveals your project summary is missing the decision the group needs to make. What would you say to show how you will use the feedback?','That helped. I’ll revise the summary to make the decision clearer.','Acknowledging a specific change closes the loop and shows that their input has practical value. Remember: connect the feedback to your next action.','intermediate',7,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000068','Peer Management','A peer asks you to endorse a proposal you have not had time to review. What would you say before offering support?','I’ll review it before offering my support.','Reviewing before endorsement keeps your support grounded in what the proposal actually contains. Remember: understand what you are endorsing.','intermediate',8,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000069','Peer Management','A peer challenges your conclusion in a meeting where the group must choose between two approaches. What would you say to make the disagreement useful?','Let’s compare our assumptions so the group can assess both views.','Comparing assumptions lets the group assess the difference instead of watching a contest over who is right. Remember: put the assumptions on the table.','intermediate',9,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000070','Peer Management','Your working relationship with a peer has become strained after several difficult handoffs. What would you say to open a mutual reset?','I’d like us to work more smoothly. Can we discuss what each of us needs?','Asking what each person needs makes the conversation reciprocal and oriented toward future cooperation. Remember: invite a reset around practical needs.','intermediate',10,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000071','Cross-Functional Management','Two departments are supporting the same project but are optimizing for different goals. What would you say to establish a shared direction?','What outcome do both teams need this project to deliver?','A common project outcome provides a reference point for resolving competing local priorities. Remember: name the outcome both teams need.','beginner',1,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000072','Cross-Functional Management','Your team''s next milestone depends on work from another department that has been delayed. What would you say to agree on a credible dependency date?','What date can your team commit to, and what support would make that possible?','A date paired with a support question accounts for the other team''s capacity and helps reveal a feasible commitment. Remember: negotiate the date and the support together.','beginner',2,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000073','Cross-Functional Management','A task crosses department boundaries, and each team assumes the other is responsible for the handoff. What would you say to clarify accountability?','Who will be accountable for this handoff from start to finish?','Naming one accountable owner prevents a shared dependency from becoming nobody''s responsibility. Remember: every handoff needs an accountable owner.','beginner',3,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000074','Cross-Functional Management','A partner team uses a technical term that different people seem to interpret differently. What would you say to establish shared understanding?','Can we define that term so we’re working from the same understanding?','Defining the term prevents teams from agreeing in words while planning different work. Remember: align on the meaning, not just the label.','beginner',4,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000075','Cross-Functional Management','A partner department changes requirements after your team has committed to the original scope. What would you say to make the implications explicit?','What impact will this have on scope and timing? Let’s update the agreement.','Updating the agreement connects a changed requirement to its cost in time or scope. Remember: revisit commitments when requirements change.','beginner',5,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000076','Cross-Functional Management','A cross-functional project has frequent status meetings but few decisions or blockers to discuss. What would you say to improve coordination without adding meetings?','Could we use a written update and meet only for decisions or blockers?','Written updates can preserve visibility while reserving discussion time for issues that need interaction. Remember: use meetings for decisions and blockers.','intermediate',6,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000077','Cross-Functional Management','A partner team raises several concerns just before a planned launch. What would you say to distinguish urgent blockers from later improvements?','What must be resolved before launch, and what can follow afterward?','Separating essential launch conditions from follow-up work helps the teams focus on the immediate decision. Remember: distinguish what must happen now from what can follow.','intermediate',7,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000078','Cross-Functional Management','A cross-team project is blocked because no department has accepted ownership of a critical task. What would you say when escalating the issue?','We need a decision on ownership by Thursday to protect the launch date.','A specific decision and deadline give the escalation a solvable purpose rather than assigning personal blame. Remember: escalate the decision that is needed.','intermediate',8,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000079','Cross-Functional Management','A handoff between teams repeatedly arrives with missing information and causes errors. What would you say to make the handoff more reliable?','Let’s agree on a checklist and an acceptance point for the handoff.','A checklist and acceptance point make completeness and responsibility visible to both teams. Remember: define what a complete handoff looks like.','intermediate',9,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000080','Cross-Functional Management','You need another function to participate in a change intended to reduce customer wait time. What would you say to connect the request with value and capacity?','This will help us reduce customer wait time. What would your team need to participate?','A shared benefit explains why participation matters, while asking about needs makes the request workable. Remember: explain the value and ask what participation requires.','intermediate',10,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000081','Self-Advocacy','A project summary leaves out the analysis you contributed to the final recommendation. What would you say to correct the record?','Could we include my work on the analysis in the final update?','A direct request for accurate inclusion makes your work visible without claiming other people''s contributions. Remember: ask for accurate recognition.','beginner',1,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000082','Self-Advocacy','You want to advance to the next level but have not discussed the criteria with your manager. What would you say to make career growth concrete?','I’d like to discuss my next level and the evidence needed to get there.','Asking for the required evidence creates a practical basis for planning and assessing progress. Remember: turn advancement into observable criteria.','beginner',2,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000083','Self-Advocacy','Your new process reduces rework, but few people outside the immediate project know about it. What would you say to share the contribution appropriately?','The new process reduced rework. I’d like to share the approach with the team.','Connecting the result with the method helps others see the value and potentially apply it. Remember: make the impact and the approach visible.','beginner',3,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000084','Self-Advocacy','You keep receiving routine tasks while the work that develops leadership skills goes to others. What would you say to request a clearer development path?','I can handle this task. I’d also like to plan for work that develops my leadership skills.','A growth request alongside the current commitment opens a future-focused discussion about work allocation. Remember: ask for development opportunities directly.','beginner',4,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000085','Self-Advocacy','You are accountable for a deadline but still need a reviewer assigned to complete the work. What would you say to request the missing resource?','To meet this deadline, I need a reviewer assigned by Tuesday.','Naming the resource and its required date connects your request to the commitment you are expected to meet. Remember: ask for the support the outcome requires.','beginner',5,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000086','Self-Advocacy','A meeting is making decisions that affect your work, but you have not been invited. What would you say to request participation?','This decision affects my work. Could I join the discussion?','Explaining the connection to your responsibilities gives a concrete reason for your involvement. Remember: connect your participation to the decision''s impact.','intermediate',6,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000087','Self-Advocacy','A leader asks what you contributed to a successful project involving several teams. What would you say to describe your role accurately?','I led the analysis that helped the team choose the lower-risk option.','A specific action and its effect distinguish your contribution without overstating ownership of the whole result. Remember: describe your contribution and its impact.','intermediate',7,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000088','Self-Advocacy','Your responsibilities have expanded, and you want to discuss whether your compensation reflects that scope. What would you say to open a factual conversation?','Could we review my compensation in light of my scope and contributions?','A request to review scope and contributions provides a basis for discussion without threats or unsupported demands. Remember: ground the request in your work and scope.','intermediate',8,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000089','Self-Advocacy','Your career development conversation has been postponed several times without a new date. What would you say to secure a concrete next step?','Can we set a date for this conversation and agree on the preparation?','A date and preparation agreement make the conversation easier to follow through on. Remember: ask for a scheduled commitment.','intermediate',9,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000090','Self-Advocacy','A visible assignment becomes available, and you hesitate to volunteer even though you have a workable approach. What would you say to express interest and readiness?','I’d like to lead this. Here is how I would approach the first milestone.','Offering the first milestone shows both motivation and a practical starting point. Remember: pair your interest with a plan.','intermediate',10,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000091','Power','You are preparing a proposal but are unsure who can approve it and who needs to be consulted. What would you say to clarify the decision process?','Who owns this decision, and who should be consulted before approval?','Understanding approval and consultation roles helps you involve the right people without relying on assumptions. Remember: know who decides and who contributes.','beginner',1,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000092','Power','A senior stakeholder makes a strong claim that could shape the decision, but no supporting evidence is discussed. What would you say to ask for a sound basis for the decision?','What evidence should we review before making this decision?','A request for evidence keeps scrutiny focused on the claim and the decision rather than the person''s status. Remember: ask for evidence with respect.','beginner',2,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000093','Power','You are asked to approve a change that falls outside the authority of your role. What would you say to clarify the boundary while helping the work move forward?','I can prepare the recommendation. The approval needs to come from the accountable owner.','Separating preparation from approval lets you contribute without assuming authority you do not have. Remember: help within your decision boundary.','beginner',3,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000094','Power','A stakeholder with approval authority is blocking the next step without clearly stating their concern. What would you say to understand the condition for support?','What concern would need to be addressed for you to support the next step?','A focused question reveals what needs to be addressed rather than treating opposition as a personal obstacle. Remember: understand the concern before seeking support.','beginner',4,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000095','Power','You have authority to make a decision that will change how another team does its day-to-day work. What would you say before making the call?','Before I decide, I’d like to hear from the people doing this work.','Hearing from people doing the work provides practical information and exposes consequences you may not see. Remember: use authority with informed input.','beginner',5,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000096','Power','A team member challenges a decision you have made and raises a possible delivery risk. What would you say to consider the challenge without losing direction?','Walk me through the risk you see. I’ll consider it before we proceed.','Inviting the risk keeps the decision open to useful evidence while preserving your responsibility to decide. Remember: listen to relevant evidence even when you own the call.','intermediate',6,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000097','Power','Two senior sponsors give conflicting directions, and your team cannot satisfy both. What would you say to bring the tradeoff to the appropriate decision owner?','Let’s bring the tradeoff to the decision owner with both perspectives included.','Transparent escalation puts both perspectives in view and clarifies who must resolve the conflict. Remember: present the tradeoff to the accountable owner.','intermediate',7,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000098','Power','A senior person implies you should stop questioning a plan because they have already decided. What would you say to keep a material delivery risk visible?','I understand the direction. I still need to flag this delivery risk.','Acknowledging the direction while flagging the risk separates respect for authority from withholding important information. Remember: respect the decision and surface the risk.','intermediate',8,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000099','Power','You join a higher-level discussion and want to contribute without speaking only to demonstrate presence. What would you say to identify where your expertise is useful?','What decision can I help clarify with the information I have?','Focusing on a decision you can clarify connects your participation to relevant information. Remember: contribute where your knowledge improves the decision.','intermediate',9,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000100','Power','You control a limited resource that several teams need, and each asks for priority. What would you say to make allocation fair and understandable?','Let’s agree on fair allocation criteria and make them visible.','Visible criteria make the allocation process more consistent and easier to explain. Remember: use transparent criteria when allocating resources.','intermediate',10,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000101','Influence','You have a new idea that requires support, but stakeholders are uncertain about committing to a full rollout. What would you say to propose a smaller next step?','Could we test this with a small pilot before making a larger commitment?','A bounded pilot lets the group learn before taking on the cost and risk of a larger commitment. Remember: use a small test to reduce uncertainty.','beginner',1,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000102','Influence','A stakeholder says your proposal is not convincing, but you do not know which part concerns them. What would you say to locate the obstacle?','Which part of the proposal feels least compelling to you?','A focused question identifies where further explanation or a revised proposal could help. Remember: find the specific point of hesitation.','beginner',2,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000103','Influence','You present a proposal to a team whose main priority is reducing turnaround time. What would you say to connect your idea to their goal?','This approach supports your goal of reducing turnaround time.','A real connection to the team''s priority makes the value relevant without inventing benefits or hiding costs. Remember: show the connection to their actual goal.','beginner',3,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000104','Influence','Your recommendation would speed delivery but create additional support work afterward. What would you say to make that tradeoff visible?','This is faster, but it increases support work. Here’s how we can manage that.','Naming the cost alongside a management plan gives stakeholders a more complete basis for deciding. Remember: present the benefit and its cost together.','beginner',4,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000105','Influence','A proposal has support from the people in the meeting, but other teams will also be affected. What would you say to identify missing perspectives?','Who else will be affected and should have a voice in this decision?','Inviting affected groups can reveal practical concerns before the decision becomes costly to change. Remember: include the people affected by the decision.','beginner',5,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000106','Influence','You have limited time to explain a proposal intended to reduce handoff errors. What would you say to make the case clearly?','The main benefit is fewer handoff errors. The evidence is in last month’s review.','One relevant benefit supported by evidence gives listeners a focused reason to consider the proposal. Remember: pair the main benefit with evidence.','intermediate',6,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000107','Influence','A team resists trying a new process because it does not fit smoothly into their current workflow. What would you say to invite a workable trial?','What would make this change easier to try in your workflow?','Asking what would make the change easier to try turns resistance into practical design input. Remember: use concerns to improve the trial.','intermediate',7,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000108','Influence','A leader expresses interest in your idea but has not committed resources or support. What would you say to request a specific sponsorship commitment?','Would you support a two-week pilot with these success measures?','A limited pilot and clear success measures make the support request easier to evaluate. Remember: ask for a concrete, measurable commitment.','intermediate',8,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000109','Influence','New evidence shows that an important assumption behind your original proposal was too optimistic. What would you say to update your recommendation?','This changes my recommendation. I suggest we adjust the scope.','Changing your position when the evidence changes shows that the outcome matters more than defending your first idea. Remember: let the evidence change your recommendation.','intermediate',9,true),
('00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000110','Influence','A discussion ends with positive comments about your proposal, but no action has been agreed. What would you say to turn interest into progress?','Can we agree on the next step and who will own it?','A next step and a named owner convert general approval into an accountable commitment. Remember: close with an action and an owner.','intermediate',10,true)
